<title>SOUTH KOREA: S.Korea big groups exceed equity investment limit.</title>
<headline>S.Korea big groups exceed equity investment limit.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-07-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Twenty-seven of South Korea's top 30 business groups had exceeded the limit on equity holdings in other companies by a combined 2.38 trillion won as of April 1, The Fair Trade Commission said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>South Korea limits equity shareholdings in other firms by subsidiaries of the nation's 30 leading conglomerates or chaebols to 25 percent of their net assets.</p>
<p>The excessive equity holdings should be disposed of by the end of next March, the commission said.</p>
<p>A commission official said 171 subsidiaries of 27 chaebols had violated the 25-percent rule.</p>
<p>The average net worth ratio -- total capital versus total assets -- of the 30 groups stood at 18.2 percent as of April 1, the lowest since 1993, the commission said.  </p>
<p>Top 10 group's financial status.</p>
<p>(Note: in trillion won, percent)</p>
<p>Company   Total Assets   Total capital  Net worth ratio</p>
<p>Hyundai	 59.3	    10.6		17.9</p>
<p>Samsung	 82.4	    14.8		17.9</p>
<p>LG		45.5	     9.6		21.1</p>
<p>Daewoo	  37.5	     9.0		24.1</p>
<p>Sungkyong     24.0	     4.9		20.4</p>
<p>Ssangyong     18.3	     3.8		20.5</p>
<p>Hanjin	  17.6	     2.5		14.3 </p>
<p>Kia	     14.5	     2.4		16.3</p>
